6.3.m:
Yudhiṣṭhira said:
All this that has been told is wondrous, O best of the twice-born;
the hosts of seers together with me have been struck with great wonder.

The venerable Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Let all the seers hear, and you too, dear Yudhiṣṭhira:
the ancient account concerning the Narmadā was spoken by the trident-wielding Lord;

Śrī Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Formerly, there was one named Citra of great splendour, beloved of Indra;
his son, O best of kings, was renowned as Pattreśvara.

Pattreśvara said:
"I am a sinful fool, whose mind had not mastered its senses;
for whatever result has been incurred, you ought to grant grace."

Uttānapāda said:
This sacred ford is very holy, consisting of all the gods, supreme;
a place more secret than the secret, never seen, never heard of, O Hara!

Īśvara said:
In ancient times there was an exceedingly valorous demon, proud of his strength;
no one on earth was like him in valor or in might.

Īśvara said:
Let all those sit down, whoever has arrived.
This Dānava was indeed slain for the sake of the gods, O Grandfather;

Īśvara said:
'You will not reach the mouth of the fire, my child, by my command;
and your dwelling, O son, shall be in my realm.'

Anasūyā said:
I am indeed blessed, meritorious in the world, praiseworthy, and ever to be revered;
for Brahmā, Viṣṇu, and Rudra have gracious faces and are auspicious.

Viṣṇu said:
Formerly, in the dialogue with Bhṛgu, dwelling in a womb was incurred;
and I do not see an end to that, O fair lady.

Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Just as you speak, O king, so my speech attains joy;
though I am beset with old age through weakness, your friendship never perishes, my dear.

Mārkaṇḍeya said:
The fiery is an ablution with ash, and the watery is by immersion;
the sacred is [reciting] “āpo hi ṣṭhā,” and the airy is regarded as cow-dust.

The brahmin said:
“You should have no doubt: the sacrifices have been performed without question,
if the Vedas are an authority, and likewise the gods and brahmins on earth;

Mārkaṇḍeya said:
Seeing this matchless wonder, the goddess was greatly astonished;
with eyes wide in amazement, she asked Śaṃkara again:

Pārvatī said:
“How could this be true, since here it is inconsistent?
Many people perform ritual ablutions here, O Maheśvara;

Māṇḍavya said:
"You are the Mother of this universe, the goddess of universal good fortune;
there is nothing in the cosmic egg devoid of you, O fair-faced one!

The Goddess said:
"Everywhere-going, I am to be seen in all beings and on every side upon the earth;
nothing whatsoever is ordained in all the worlds without me.

The baṭu said:
'Do not grieve or express regret for the sake of food, O bulls among twice-born;
go home duly, since it has already been prepared.'

casting away the sin committed across seven births, goes to the abode of Śiva.
Having performed a śrāddha according to the rule, he becomes debt-free to the ancestors;
he should feed brahmins according to his means and give them a priestly gift.

By building a temple for the god according to his means, one becomes a king on earth;
and one should gratify the Supreme Lord with songs, dances, and holy recitations.
Thus ends the two hundred and twenty-sixth chapter, named the Description of the Greatness of the Sacred Ford of Vimaleśvara, in the Revākhaṇḍa of the fifth section, the Āvantyakhaṇḍa, in the glorious Skanda Mahāpurāṇa, a compendium of eighty-one thousand verses.

Mārkaṇḍeya said:
These have been told to you in brief, focusing on the prominent ones;
I am unable to declare in detail the number of sacred fords, O son of Pāṇḍu.

Yudhiṣṭhira said:
Tell me the procedure, the restraints, and the observances;
when one goes for the sake of expiation, tell me what the procedure is.

two Pippaleśvara sacred fords known as Māṇḍavyeśvara;
and two Dvīpeśvaras, the uplifter of Bhṛgu’s line said likewise;
two Uttareśvara sacred fords, and a pair of Aśokeśas; // 231.24

Likewise, he spoke of seven sacred fords for Brahmā, for the attainment of perfection;
in three of them, Brahmā is worshipped, and the other four are Brahmeśas.
Twenty-eight have been declared by me according to number and order. // 231.30

gopāḥ = cowherds; te sarve = all of them; vrajamaṇḍalavāsinaḥ = dwellers in the circle of Vraja;
nihatya = having slain; rākṣasān sarvān = all the demons;
golokaṃ tu tadā yayuḥ = then they went to Goloka.
The brahmin named Śatānanda, endowed with great wisdom, [by the efficacy of observing the vow of Satyanārāyaṇa] became the brahmin named Sudāmā in another birth, and having meditated upon Lord Śrī Kṛṣṇa in that birth, he attained liberation. 19.
The woodcutter became Guharāja, the king of the Bhillas, and in his next birth, having served Lord Śrī Rāma, he attained liberation. 20.
The great king Ulkāmukha [in another birth] became King Daśaratha, who, having worshipped Śrī Raṅganātha (Viṣṇu), ultimately attained Vaikuṇṭha. 21.
